verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"bd-cfd90a4600fd0086","kind":"behavior"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261001-BNZG","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.behaviorDiff (differential harness run at base and head)","note":"Recorded difference bd-cfd90a4600fd0086 is classified intended (SW-REQ-261001-BNZG).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js:stripJsonc","shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml:parseMenuJsonc"],"scenario":"in a running Quickshell (QV4), load default/omarchy/omarchy-menu.jsonc and a user extension with a leading BOM, a mid-file BOM and NBSP-indented lines through Menu.qml FileView onLoaded: are the row counts 346 and the user rows as under node, and what is the parse time vs the base regex version?"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Every test runs MenuModel.js under node. In production, Menu.qml calls parseMenuJsonc from each FileView onLoaded (Menu.qml:1045, 1056) inside QV4. The scanner relies on /\\s/ Unicode tables and does a per-character string concatenation. In node (rerun on this mirror), stripJsonc takes about 0.26 ms vs 0.07 ms for the regexes on the 55 KB default menu, and about 23 ms vs 1.3 ms on a 1.1 MB file; parseMenuJsonc as a whole is about 0.6 ms vs 0.3 ms on the default menu (single-machine timings, noisy). QV4 interprets or JITs differently, and no test measures it there. The PR body states that Quickshell's FileView already drops a BOM at the very start of the file, and the behaviour-diff harness models that (its comment says it was checked live under Quickshell 0.3.1); no in-scope test records it, and a BOM later in the file still reaches the parser. The baseline's menu-live suite runs menu-acceptance-test.sh in quickshell with a user JSONC that has trailing commas and commas inside strings (menu-acceptance-test.sh:172), and the package lists it among the tests run, but links it to no parser requirement, so its outcome is not evidence here, and it feeds no BOM, Unicode space or inline comment and measures no parse time.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":1045},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":1056},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js","kind":"file","line":39},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-acceptance-test.sh","kind":"file","line":172},{"id":"SW-REQ-261001-BNZG","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: does the scanner behave and perform the same under Quickshell's QV4 engine as under node?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"QV4 witness: parse the default menu and a BOM/NBSP user extension inside Quickshell\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js:parseMenuJsonc","SW-REQ-260922-7NPE"],"scenario":"upgrade with an existing ~/.config/omarchy/extensions/omarchy-menu.jsonc that has an inline // comment and overrides a shipped id: base shows 0 user rows and the shipped row; does head replace the shipped row with the user override?"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"A user file with an inline // comment, or a Unicode space or BOM between tokens, used to contribute 0 rows; the shipped menu kept working. After this PR the same file parses, and its ids override shipped ones per SW-REQ-260922-7NPE. That is the PR's purpose, but an upgrade can change a user's menu without any edit on their part (e.g. an old, forgotten override now becomes active).","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":1056},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-7NPE","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260928-C8W1","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: do user extensions that silently loaded as empty before now load and override shipped entries after upgrade?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Upgrade witness: inline-commented user extension overriding a shipped id\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"I ran both: a CR-only file with a // comment (`// c\\r{...}`) and a /* */ block comment give 0 rows at base and at head. The scanner ends a comment only at \\n and has no block-comment state. The PR's docs/menu.md now says block comments are not supported. These KIs pre-date the PR; their evidence on the mirror is still known_issue_reproduced.","refs":[{"id":"proof/known-issues/KI-MENU-JSONC-CR-LINE-ENDINGS.yaml","kind":"file","line":1},{"id":"proof/known-issues/KI-MENU-JSONC-STRIP-GAPS.yaml","kind":"file","line":1},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-E4J2","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"KI-MENU-JSONC-CR-LINE-ENDINGS and KI-MENU-JSONC-STRIP-GAPS remain open; the PR does not change them","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"effects_unavailable: review.effects covers C/C++ only, so callers, gained effects, new mutable state and call-level requirement impact are not in the package for this JS change. The scope approximations say no uncodified connection could be detected, so 'No spec-gap candidates' proves nothing here. By reading: stripJsonc/parseMenuJsonc add no callees, no I/O, and no state beyond local variables.","refs":[{"id":"review.effects.approximations","kind":"check"},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js","kind":"file","line":2}],"severity":"info","title":"Change effects not analyzed; scope analysis bounds acknowledged","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"Each of the 135 compat_change_unapproved disclosures prevents readiness until a reviewer or above approves it under the approved policy @ 1e433732, or the author fixes it. The next decision states the exact count (\"Approve each of the 135 backward-compatibility changes\"). The auditor (buger, who is also this PR's author) has recorded notes on 94 of them (11 per finding), assessing each as acceptable with the requirement or issue it follows. The 41 findings whose ids are new this round carry no auditor note yet. The engine shows those notes as informational, not approvals, and they resolve nothing. My reading that each difference is intended is commentary, not an approval either.","refs":[{"id":"review.compat","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"All 135 compatibility findings stay unapproved; only a human reviewer can approve them","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The body cites evaluated merge e3848ce on quattro 393a43d (62 passed) and says the shipped menu parses to 344 rows. This package evaluates af2d9e82 on quattro 035ce29f (69 passed), where the shipped menu has 346 rows at both base and head, with identical row data (I ran it). The body's claim of an unchanged shipped menu still holds; only the numbers moved.","refs":[{"id":"default/omarchy/omarchy-menu.jsonc","kind":"file","line":235},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js","kind":"file","line":87}],"severity":"info","title":"The PR body's evaluation line and row count predate the upstream refresh","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:d1fc4bf87c8c2f94f0097174f73e5144f2e2f0fc7b797879e18a3e865fade9b4","packageIdentity":"epkg-2b51950722ee8ea887c1a1bdf342f8a9e20dea91dc07f6a530ee7b9a199340d9","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"Small parser change. Each fix has differential and observed test evidence, all 127 differences match a requirement, and the fuzz found nothing worse than the base. But 135 compatibility changes are unapproved (prevents ready), the production QV4 runtime and its parse cost are untested, and upgraded users' previously-ignored extension files will start to apply."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":31,"rationale":"Settles whether production QV4 matches node on /\\s/, BOM delivery via FileView.text(), and parse latency.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":1045},{"id":"SW-REQ-261001-BNZG","kind":"requirement"}],"title":"QV4 witness: parse the default menu and a BOM/NBSP user extension inside Quickshell"},{"forClaim":32,"rationale":"Shows base (override ignored) vs head (override active), so the release note can warn users.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":1056},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-7NPE","kind":"requirement"}],"title":"Upgrade witness: inline-commented user extension overriding a shipped id"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #13968 (ff77cd02 + b2dae8db, live head) replaces stripJsonc's two regexes with a string-aware scanner and makes parseMenuJsonc return no rows for an array root. It fixes #13250 (a comma before } or ] in a string was eaten), #13492 (an array root became rows \"0\",\"1\") and #13493 (an inline // emptied the file). b2dae8db also reads JS whitespace outside strings (BOM, NBSP, Unicode spaces, VT, FF) as a space. Mirror commit 1d6557ec applies it onto baseline quattro-proof 1e433732 (upstream quattro 035ce29f); the next 14 commits are our proof work (tip af2d9e82). Advisory audit; not an approval.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** Changed-code coverage: 17/17 decision lines, 34/34 decisions. Test results are current, 69/69 passed (run-1084a010). All obligation cells of the 6 parser requirements are observed_pass. I ran base 1e433732 and head: each issue reproduces at base, not at head; 200k fuzzed inputs: none worse.\n\n**Compatibility.** Readiness is not_assessed. Next: human_judgement (compat_changes_unapproved), \"Approve each of the 135 backward-compatibility changes\", by a reviewer (approved policy @ 1e433732). 135 compat_change_unapproved disclosures prevent readiness (136 findings, 1 fixed). All 127 differences are classified intended: 123 data inputs with JS whitespace go from parse error to parsed (BNZG); 4 are the issue reproducers. 41 have new ids only because the shipped menu gained two upstream rows (#10367): 346 rows, not 344. The 5 test_diff and 3 unapproved removed_behavior findings are intended too; auditor notes are not approvals.\n\n**Questions for a human.** (1) Does Quickshell's QV4 engine match node on /\\s/ and on cost (in node stripJsonc is ~4x slower on the default menu, ~17x on 1.1 MB)? No test runs QV4. (2) User extension files that loaded empty before (inline comment, Unicode space) now load and can override shipped entries.\n\n**Traceability.** stripJsonc and parseMenuJsonc are traced at function level: E4J2, 3T3F, 66FW, C8W1, BNZG, BMFE (4 added; 3 modified, with PPDW). 23 more are code_changed only via file-level test traces; their 23 not_linked cells have no tagged test (4 more are suppressed). No spec-gap candidates; change effects were not analyzed (effects_unavailable).\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** KI-MENU-JSONC-CR-LINE-ENDINGS and -STRIP-GAPS stay open. The upstream refresh changes no file this PR touches. No gate fails; 13 checks were skipped. The PR body cites the last evaluation (e3848ce, 62 passed, 344 rows)."}
